  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Training Lay Counselors to Deliver Mental Health Services</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">recent data estimates that more than 260 million people globally live with depression and over 280 million suffer from anxiety disorders. poverty and mental health are inextricably linked and prevalent in developing countries where a critical shortage of mental health professionals means that people living in poverty lack access to mental health care. to make mental health care more accessible in zimbabwe, an evidence-based, scalable model where government nurses and ngo staff train community health workers (chws) as lay counselors will provide cognitive behavioral therapy with a focus on problem-solving therapy (pst). these trained chwsknown as grandmothers and grandfathers screen people seeking mental health support and provide them with six sessions of pst and access to an optional peer support group.</narrative>
  </description>
